      forward: "⚖️ Balanced, DOWN from +2 to +1 - and since that is a second cut in two days, here is the fact that did it rather than a restatement. Yesterday\'s entry set the test in numbers: at or above 4.1% validates Monday and opens ANZ\'s 4.85% path on November 3, at or below 3.5% makes the hike a policy error. 4.0% resolved neither, so the rule as written did not decide it. The line that decided it was the third one on the list: trimmed mean m/m 0.2% against 0.3% forecast and 0.5% prior, trimmed mean y/y pinned at 3.6% for a third month. That measure annualises near 2.4% on the month - inside the band - and it is the one the Board sets policy on, which is why the ratePaths entry has flipped from hike to hold. Be precise about the leg, because it is not the same one as yesterday: yesterday policy held and price failed; today price failed again through 0.70 and the forward policy leg narrowed too, because the +2 was explicitly reserving room for a November 3 repricing the market had not taken and this print removes its trigger. Most economists already had 4.60% as the peak. It stays at +1 rather than 0 because nothing here is dovish in level terms - highest policy rate on the board by 60bp, highest ten-year of the majors, a 4.0% headline against a 2-3% target and guidance that still says raise further if needed. The asymmetry has simply inverted. What would restore +2: the September-quarter CPI in late October, the print CBA explicitly conditioned November on, or a Gulf re-escalation that puts the fuel back. What would take it to 0 or below: the energy unwind continuing, because Brent at 96.573 from 107.204 in a day, -6.31% on the week after the Yanbu recovery and roughly 3.5 million barrels a day restored around the strait, decays the imported half of a 4.0% headline without the RBA touching anything - and NAB\'s slower warning then applies, that Australia may finish tightening before its peers and be among the first forced to reverse, with the housing leg the Board itself broadened to falling prices in \'most\' capital cities as the place it starts. One flag: this page has still not analysed Governor Bullock\'s Monday press conference, which wire coverage described as dulling the hike\'s message and which is where AUD/USD actually lost 0.70."
