      drivers: "TE re-verified: the overnight rate is 2.25%, held on September 2 for a seventh consecutive decision. Pricing corrected: 59% on overnight index swaps as of September 25 for an October 28 hike, implying +15bp, described as close to a coin flip tilted to a hike, up from roughly 94% priced for a hold before September 2 - with RBC's Claire Fan and most bank economists still expecting a hold through 2026 and a first-quarter 2027 hike. Macklem called the global energy shock the biggest tailwind to higher prices and said inflationary risks had sharpened since July; the governing council noted that the longer high oil persists the more likely it spreads into other goods and services. Headline CPI 3.0%, core rising to 2.4% from 2.3%, trimmed mean 1.9%, Q2 GDP 0.8% q/q and 1.1% y/y from 0.1%, August retail sales +1.3% m/m after -0.7%, unemployment 6.4%, manufacturing PMI 53.0. Canada 10-year 3.9670, +4.2bp. Against it: August employment -41.7k after +75.1k, services PMI 46.8 and composite 47.8 - the only contracting readings on this board - wage growth 3.2% and falling, and USD/CAD at 1.41870 up 0.88% on the week and 2.40% on the month while Brent rose 18.47%.",
      forward: "⚖️ Balanced, and the score holds at +1 while the verdict moves from STRENGTHENING to MIXED - because the two things that justified the stronger label yesterday have both failed verification. The first is the number: this page carried 68% priced for October 28 and that is not verifiable; the best-sourced figure is 59% on OIS as of September 25, a coin flip, and the economists who cover this Bank are on the other side of it. The second is transmission: Canada is the purest oil beneficiary on this board, Brent is up 18.47% on the month, and the Canadian dollar has lost 2.40% against the US dollar over the same four weeks. When the cleanest fundamental tailwind available to a currency does not show up in its price for a month, the honest conclusion is that something else is dominating - here, the dollar's dual role and Canada's own contracting service sector. So +1 now rests on the inflation-and-terms-of-trade legs, not on the pricing leg, and it is stated that way. Today at 12:30Z supplies the first test and the bar has moved against it: monthly GDP has had its forecast cut to 0.0% from 0.3% previous, and a flat or negative print reasserts the domestic story, takes the coin flip toward a hold and would justify cutting CAD to 0. Then the Business Outlook Survey on October 19 and the Monetary Policy Report on October 28. What would take CAD back to +2: a GDP beat today combined with a Hormuz escalation holding Brent above 110 into the MPR, which pushes October pricing decisively past 50% and finally gets the currency paid for the barrel. One internal tension on this page is worth naming rather than hiding - a USOIL LONG alongside a USD/CAD LONG is coherent only because the barrel is not reaching the currency, and if that changes both positions need revisiting rather than one."
