      drivers: "TE re-verified: the ECB deposit facility rate is 2.50%, raised 25bp on September 10 from 2.25%, main refinancing rate 2.65%. From Lagarde's introductory statement at the European Parliament ECON hearing on September 28, published by the ECB: 'we do not react to energy prices, we react if we see risks of higher energy prices becoming embedded in inflation'; 'we do not see evidence at this stage of energy prices feeding into higher wages'; 'since our last meeting long-term interest rates have risen notably, which will slow growth and reduce pass-through by more than projected'. She described the stance as 'a measured response' and placed the ECB on the 'middle path', giving no guidance for October 29. The inflation side: headline accelerated to 3.2% in August from 2.9%, energy inflation to 14.3% from 10.3%, core 2.4%, and Friday's flash carries a 3.7% forecast with core 2.5%; German preliminary CPI tomorrow is forecast at 0.5% m/m against 0.2%. Her evidence: negotiated wage growth 2.44% from 2.56%, headline wage growth 3.0% from 3.4%, July retail sales -0.6% m/m, consumer confidence -16.5 from -15.5. Activity is fine: Q2 GDP 0.6% q/q and 1.2% y/y, September composite PMI 53.1 from 52.0 with French business activity in growth for the first time in ten months, unemployment 6.4%. EUR/USD 1.13595, -0.10% today, -2.22% on the month. Bund 3.6434, France 4.7760, Italy 4.6120.",
      forward: "⚖️ Range, and DOWN from +1 to 0 on the most durable change on this page. The euro's +1 was built on accelerating inflation opening a route to a faster ECB. Yesterday the ECB's president closed that route in advance, in her own words, at a primary source: the Bank does not react to energy prices, it sees no evidence of them reaching wages, and higher long-term yields will do more of the slowing than its September projection assumed. Read the consequence carefully, because it is asymmetric rather than simply bearish. A hot euro-area print - and Friday's flash is forecast at 3.7% - is now a growth negative without being a policy positive, because the reaction function has been pre-announced as passive and the bond market has been named as the tightening instrument. That is the definition of a currency you do not want to own into its own inflation data, and it is the opposite of how a 3.7% print would normally trade. The comparison is what makes it tradeable: every other energy-exposed bank here is being dragged toward tightening by the same barrel - Bailey 80-84% priced on an explicitly energy-conditional hike, Macklem naming the shock himself into a coin-flip October, Breman warning oil could push New Zealand inflation past track - while the ECB has said it will not join them. So trade EUR against those reaction functions rather than on euro-area data: EUR/GBP SHORT is the purest expression, EUR/USD SHORT the highest-conviction one. What invalidates this, and Lagarde left the hatch open herself in the word 'embedded': a German preliminary print tomorrow well above 0.5% m/m followed by a flash above 3.7% on Friday, with any sign of wage spillover in the detail, forces her off 'measured' - and negotiated wages are the series to watch, because at 2.44% and falling they are currently her strongest argument. She speaks again today at 11:00Z and Thursday at 13:30Z, and the accounts of the September meeting land October 8. The other upside risk is relative: the euro is the biggest beneficiary on this board if a Hormuz breakthrough collapses the barrel, since it is the currency whose central bank never positioned for the shock in the first place."
